 By Colin Demarest &middot; Sep 09, 2026 
 
 
 Long weekend. Shorter newsletter.

 I'm interviewing Pentagon chief technology officer Emil Michael onstage tomorrow . Send me your questions! 🧠 Situational awareness: Chariot Defense will announce its board of advisers this morning. It includes retired Gen. James Rainey and retired Brig. Gen. Samuel Peterson; Ellen Lord, formerly the Pentagon's top weapons buyer; and Stephen Rodriguez , a defense investor at DCVC.

 Stay awhile and listen: An Allen Control Systems expansion, a Palantir-Anduril combo and some Space Force fits.

 Today's newsletter is 1,674 words, a 6.5-minute read. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: A sizzling summer 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Troops assigned to Joint Task Force-Southern Border down a drone with a laser. Photo: Daniel Huerta/DVIDS

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The summer of love happened in 1967. The summer of lasers is happening right now. 

 Both upset the status quo. The big picture: These weapons, so often associated with science fiction or Congressional Research Service reports, are clawing into reality after decades of investment and experimentation.

 Recent progress and other milestones include:

 The U.S. Army awarding a first-ever high-energy laser production contract, worth $465 million, to AeroVironment . The company will deliver dozens of Locust X3s over the coming years. Troops on the U.S.-Mexico border zapping at least 11 cartel-linked drones — what the Laser Wars blog described as the Pentagon's "first known 'kill'" with such a system. Ground A and Inleap Photonics, German startups building laser countermeasures, raising €9 million and €20 million, respectively. (That's almost $34 million total.) Chief of Naval Operations Adm. Daryl Caudle committing a service-wide memo to directed energy, arguing that the "question is no longer whether these systems work — it is how quickly we can field them across the fleet and place them in the hands of sailors." U.S. Air Forces in Europe and Air Forces Africa saying that it was using Boeing's Compact Laser Weapon System in "locations across the theater" and not in "isolation." Lockheed Martin and nLight winning contracts for the Joint Laser Weapon System, with the ceiling pegged at $847 million . Initial prototypes will be 150 kW strong; future versions will hit 300–500 kW. And both Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth and Pentagon chief technology officer Emil Michael attending a laser- and microwave-weapon demonstration in New Mexico . Friction point: The promise of lasers is fantastic: dirt-cheap cost per shot, near-endless magazines and the opportunity to save bigger guns, such as Patriot interceptors or Standard Missiles, for juicier targets.

 But they are no panacea. Lasers can be thrown off by atmospheric conditions, like fog and sand, are reliant on power production, which can be disrupted (or take up precious space aboard a warship), and require line of sight, which means proximity to the thing trying to kill you. Experts have also warned of supply-chain vulnerabilities , including foreign and sole-source dependencies. Follow the money: The Trump administration's fiscal 2027 budget blueprint included more than $2 billion for directed-energy research, development and testing.

 That's markedly more spending — proposed, at least — than in prior years, according to the Government Accountability Office's math . At least 31 directed-energy initiatives were underway across the U.S. military in 2024. What we're watching: CBS News reported "a roughly 75% decrease in the number of drones probing the southern border in the Rio Grande Valley" following employment of lasers.

 The bottom line: This feels like an inflection point.

