 4. ICYMI: Palantir production 
 
 By Colin Demarest 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 A basic variant of TITAN, seen at an Anduril Industries facility in early 2025. Photo: Colin Demarest/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The U.S. Army has inked contracts worth a total of $192 million with Palantir Technologies and Anduril Industries for the production of eight Tactical Intelligence Targeting Access Nodes.

 Why it matters: State-of-the-art TITAN trucks are cornerstones of the service's deep-sensing and long-range precision fires goals — in other words, the ability to find, track, shoot and kill more accurately from farther away.

 They are also key contributors to the Joint All-Domain Command and Control networking nirvana the Pentagon has for years sought to achieve. Driving the news: Four advanced variants and four basic variants of TITAN will be delivered over the next year and a half, according to the Army . 

 The former is based on the Family of Medium Tactical Vehicles. The latter is based on the Joint Light Tactical Vehicle. Additional configurations are being considered. An Infantry Squad Vehicle version was previously spotted at the Association of the U.S. Army conference. The finished products will join prototypes the service already has on hand. (An early version was delivered to Joint Base Lewis-McChord, Washington, in 2024.) How it works: TITAN trades sticky notes for artificial intelligence. It hoovers up battlefield information — including from space — and parses it to give troops quicker, sharper insights.

